  <article class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <div class="space-y-6 text-warm-700 leading-relaxed">
      <p>If you visit any Chinese museum, you will see jade. Lots of it. But unless you know what you are looking at, the display cases can feel like a blur of greenish-white geometric objects. Here is a practical guide to the two most important types of Neolithic Chinese jade — <strong>cong</strong> and <strong>bi</strong> — and what archaeologists think they actually meant.</p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cong (琮): The Squared Tube with a Round Hole</h2>
      <p>A cong is a hollow jade tube with a square outer cross-section and a round inner hole. The most famous examples come from the <a href="/liangzhu-ancient-city" class="text-accent hover:underline">Liangzhu culture</a> (c. 3300–2300 BCE). The outer corners are typically carved with a stylized face pattern: two concentric circles for eyes, a horizontal bar for a nose or mouth. This face motif appears on cong of all sizes, from a few centimeters to over 30 cm tall.</p>
      <p>No one knows for certain what cong were used for. The leading theory is that they were ritual objects associated with power and possibly used in ceremonies connecting the earthly realm (the square) with the heavens (the circle). They are almost exclusively found in elite tombs, sometimes arranged around the body of the deceased. If you see a cong at a museum, you are looking at one of the oldest symbols of authority in Chinese civilization.</p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bi (璧): The Jade Disc</h2>
      <p>A bi is a flat jade disc with a small hole in the center. Bi are older than cong — they appear as early as the <a href="/dawenkou-site" class="text-accent hover:underline">Dawenkou culture</a> (c. 4300 BCE) and continue through the Liangzhu period and beyond. Unlike cong, bi are usually undecorated, relying on the quality of the jade and the precision of the disc itself for their impact.</p>
      <p>Bi are typically interpreted as symbols of heaven or the sun, but they may have served multiple functions: status markers, ritual gifts, and possibly a form of wealth storage. In some burials, dozens of bi are stacked around the body, suggesting they were important grave goods for the elite.</p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to See the Best Neolithic Jade</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc list-inside">
        <li><strong>Liangzhu Museum</strong> (Hangzhou): The world's best collection of cong, including the famous "King of Cong" at nearly 50 cm tall.</li>
        <li><strong>Shanghai Museum</strong>: Excellent Neolithic jade gallery with representative pieces from multiple cultures.</li>
        <li><strong>National Museum of China</strong> (Beijing): The jade gallery covers Neolithic through Qing, with strong Liangzhu and Hongshan sections.</li>
        <li><strong>Henan Museum</strong> (Zhengzhou): Includes jade from Henan's Neolithic and early Bronze Age sites.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Jade Mattered</h2>
      <p>The key thing to understand about Neolithic Chinese jade is that it was incredibly difficult to work. Jade cannot be carved with metal tools — it must be ground and polished with abrasive sand over weeks or months. A single cong might represent hundreds of hours of labor. The people who owned these objects controlled access to both the raw material and the skilled labor to shape it. Jade was not just beautiful. It was power made visible.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-muted">Sources: Underhill, Anne P. <em>A Companion to Chinese Archaeology</em> (2013); Liu, Li and Chen, Xingcan. <em>The Archaeology of China</em> (2012); Liangzhu Museum exhibition labels.</p>
